---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Rockhopper Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Rockhopper Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Eudyptes chrysocome (Southern Rockhopper), Eudyptes moseleyi (Northern Rockhopper), Eudyptes filholi (Eastern Rockhopper) - often considered distinct species.</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 50-70 cm (20-28 inches) tall, weighing 2-3 kg (4.4-6.6 lbs).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Found on various subantarctic islands, with different subspecies having distinct breeding ranges. Southern Rockhoppers are widespread, Northern Rockhoppers mainly breed in the South Atlantic, and Eastern Rockhoppers around New Zealand subantarctic islands.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Endangered (Northern), Vulnerable (Southern and Eastern)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>Rockhopper Penguins primarily feed on krill, other crustaceans, small fish, and squid, varying their diet based on location and availability.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Rockhopper Penguins are known for their energetic hopping over rocks on their breeding grounds. They form large, noisy colonies on rocky slopes. They have complex social interactions involving vocalizations and displays. Like other crested penguins, they typically lay two eggs, with the first being smaller and often failing to survive.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They are named for their habit of hopping over rocks.</li>
                        <li>Rockhopper Penguins have distinctive spiky black and yellow crests.</li>
                        <li>There is ongoing debate about whether the different populations represent distinct species.</li>
                        <li>Many populations have experienced significant declines in recent decades, likely due to climate change and changes in ocean conditions affecting their food sources.</li>
                        <li>Conservation efforts are focused on understanding and mitigating the threats to their populations.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>